- email domain blocklist: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/DisposableEmailValidator.java`, method `isValid` (19-30). `BLOCKED_DOMAINS = {mailinator.com, tempmail.com, guerrillamail.com}` (line 16-17). Annotation: `.../rest/validation/DisposableEmailValidation.java`.
- postcode: used in `Households.idFor` (household hash), `CustomerCode.of` (region), soft-match, and validated by `/home/daniel/sandbox/.../rest/validation/PostcodeValidator.java` (+ `PostcodeValidation.java`).

---

## 6. Service class(es) handling owner creation

Owner creation orchestration is in the controller `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (above). The actual persistence service:

- Interface: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicService.java` (`saveOwner`, `findAllOwners`, etc.)
- Impl: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`

The service itself contains no identity/duplicate logic — all of that lives in the v1 controller helper classes. (There is also a `v2/OwnerRestControllerV2.java` which does not appear to carry the identity/duplicate rules.)

Related helper classes in the same package: `IdempotencyKeys.java`, `BusinessDays.java`, `BulkSignups.java`, `CityCodes.java`, `MembershipLevels.java` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/MembershipLevels.java`).

---

## 7. Test files

Primary: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java`

- `coreIdentityKeyIsSha256Hex` (lines 14-19): fetches an owner and asserts `identityKey` matches `[0-9a-f]{64}`.
- `coreRejectsDuplicateIdentity` (lines 21-28): creates an owner, re-posts an identical copy, expects `status().isConflict()` (409).

Class-level javadoc (lines 9-11) defines the intended v2 rule:
> `identityKey = SHA-256 hex over (normalizedTelephone | lowerEmail | soundex(lastName)); duplicate detection (409) uses it.`

Supporting base: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` — helpers `createOwner`/`createOwnerOk` (155-162), `fetchOwner` (200-204), `deleteOwner` (soft-delete note, 195-197), `structuredOwner` (136-142), `uniqueEmail` (106-108), and reference hash helpers `sha256hex` (282-294) / `shaHex` (296-299).

Also: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AuditLogCapture.java` (matched the audit/hash grep, not directly duplicate-related).

---

## Notable discrepancy (implementation vs. test spec)

The test `coreIdentityKeyIsSha256Hex` and the class javadoc expect `identityKey` to be:
1. a field returned on the owner JSON (`r.get("identityKey")`), and
2. a full 64-char SHA-256 hex over `normalizedTelephone | lowerEmail | soundex(lastName)`.

The actual implementation (`Identities.identityKey`, Identities.java:20-23):
- returns a plain pipe-delimited lowercase string (`telephone|email|householdId`), not a SHA-256 hash;
- uses `householdId`, not `soundex(lastName)` — no soundex exists anywhere in `src/main`;
- is never assigned to the Owner and is absent from both the Owner model (no `identityKey` field/getter) and `openapi.yml` (no `identityKey` property).

So `coreIdentityKeyIsSha256Hex` would fail (no `identityKey` field is serialized). `coreRejectsDuplicateIdentity` can still pass because 409 is driven by the internal `Identities.isDuplicate` string comparison, which is order-consistent for identical telephone/email/last+postcode. I did not modify anything.

